course: dictionary
lesson: 5747
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Louvre is one of these words. In «Picture Dad at the Louvre», the capital L and the place tell you it is a name: the Louvre is a famous art museum in Paris, France.
explain: Now look at «louvre doors». There is no capital letter, and the word describes a door, not a place. A louvre is one of a set of sloping slats that lets air in but keeps out rain or direct light. Ask yourself: is the sentence talking about a place or a thing? That question solves most cases.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The Mona Lisa hangs behind glass in the Louvre» makes sense with the museum meaning. «The shed has louvres, so the air can flow through» makes sense with the slats meaning. If the swap sounds silly, try the other meaning.
fact: A louvre is one of a set of sloping slats in a door, window or shutter.
fact: Louvres let air in but keep out rain or direct light.
fact: The Louvre, with a capital L, is a famous art museum in Paris, France, in an old royal palace.
fact: The Louvre is home to the «Mona Lisa», painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the early 1500s.
fact: In French, the Louvre is said «LOO-vruh».
exercise: «The Louvre is one of the most visited museums in the world.» In this sentence, what is the Louvre? | A type of petrol station | A kind of cupboard | *An art museum in Paris, France | A set of sloping slats in a window
exercise: «The cupboard had louvre doors so the air could flow through.» What is a louvre here? | A pile of paintings | *One of a set of sloping slats that lets air in | A petrol station | A famous museum
exercise: Which clue tells you that Louvre is the name of a place? | *The capital L at the start of the word | The word cupboard | The word through | The word doors
test: «Chen squeezed into a crowd of phones to see the Louvre in Paris.» What is the Louvre in this sentence? | A type of car | *A famous art museum in Paris | A slatted window | A cupboard in a shed
test: «Shutters with louvres let air in but keep out direct light.» What do the louvres do? | Turn the shutter into a museum | *Let air in but keep out direct light | Make the room dark and cold | Show paintings to visitors
exam: «The louvre on the shed door lets air in.» Which meaning fits? | A petrol station | A museum in Paris | A famous painting | *One of a set of sloping slats
